### What is static binding vs dynamic binding?

Binding determines which method implementation is called. Static binding makes this decision at compile time. Dynamic binding makes it at runtime.

### Which methods use static binding?

Four types of method calls use static binding: private, static, final, and overloaded.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class Account {
    // Static binding: private (not inherited, no vtable entry)
    private void audit() { System.out.println("Account audit"); }

    // Static binding: static (class-level, no vtable)
    public static String getPolicy() { return "Standard Policy"; }

    // Static binding: final (sealed, no override possible)
    public final String getAccountId() { return "ACC-BASE"; }

    // Dynamic binding: instance method (can be overridden, vtable dispatch)
    public String getDescription() { return "Generic Account"; }

    // Static binding: overloaded methods (compiler picks by arg types)
    public void deposit(BigDecimal amount) { }
    public void deposit(BigDecimal amount, String note) { }

    public void callAudit() { audit(); } // private -> static binding
}

public class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    @Override public String getDescription() { return "Savings Account"; }
    public static String getPolicy() { return "Savings Policy"; } // hiding
}
```

### Which methods use dynamic binding?

Overridden instance methods use dynamic binding. The JVM resolves them at runtime through the vtable.

```java
Account ref = new SavingsAccount();

// Dynamic binding (runtime: actual type = SavingsAccount)
ref.getDescription();
// Compiler: Account has getDescription()? Yes -> valid.
// Runtime: JVM checks actual type -> SavingsAccount -> vtable -> SavingsAccount.getDescription()
// Result: "Savings Account" (Child's version)

// Static binding (compile-time: reference type = Account)
ref.getPolicy();        // "Standard Policy" (Account's static method)
ref.getAccountId();     // "ACC-BASE" (Account's final method)
ref.callAudit();        // "Account audit" (private method, static binding)
ref.deposit(amount);    // compiler picks overload 1 at compile time
```

### How does the vtable work internally?

The JVM creates a vtable for each class at class loading time. The vtable maps method signatures to actual method implementations.

```java
// Account vtable:
// getDescription() -> Account.getDescription
// deposit(BigDecimal) -> Account.deposit

// SavingsAccount vtable:
// getDescription() -> SavingsAccount.getDescription  (overridden)
// deposit(BigDecimal) -> Account.deposit              (inherited, same)

// When: Account ref = new SavingsAccount()
// ref.getDescription() -> JVM looks at actual object -> SavingsAccount
// -> checks SavingsAccount vtable -> getDescription() -> SavingsAccount.getDescription()

// private, static, final methods are NOT in the vtable:
// -> resolved at compile time -> no vtable lookup -> faster
```

### Why does Parent p = new Child(); p.method() call Child's version?

Because the compiler generates `invokevirtual Parent.method()` (valid: Parent has the method). At runtime, the JVM checks p's actual object type (Child), looks up Child's vtable, finds Child.method(), and calls it. The variable type (Parent) determined compile-time validity. The actual type (Child) determined runtime dispatch.

### How do the bytecode instructions differ?

| Instruction | Binding | Used for |
|------------|---------|----------|
| `invokevirtual` | Dynamic (vtable) | Instance method calls |
| `invokeinterface` | Dynamic (itable) | Interface method calls |
| `invokestatic` | Static | Static method calls |
| `invokespecial` | Static | Constructors, super.method(), private methods |

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is assuming all method calls go through dynamic binding.

```java
// WRONG assumption: private methods dispatch dynamically
// Private methods use invokespecial: static binding. No vtable.

// WRONG assumption: static methods dispatch dynamically
// Account ref = new SavingsAccount();
// ref.getPolicy() returns "Standard Policy" (Account's version)
// Static methods use invokestatic: no vtable lookup.

// WRONG assumption: final methods have overhead from vtable lookup
// The JVM can optimize final methods because no override exists.
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- All instance method calls go through dynamic binding (invokevirtual) except private, which uses invokespecial.
- HotSpot can devirtualize monomorphic call sites (only one implementation exists) and inline the method body for performance.
- The JIT compiler uses class hierarchy analysis (CHA) to determine if a virtual call can be devirtualized.
- Interface method calls use `invokeinterface` with an itable (interface method table) instead of vtable, which is slightly slower.
